<item>
  <title>Welcome to Fire Service Data Science</title>
  <link>https://titofire.github.io/fire-data-science-blog/posts/welcome/</link>
  <description><![CDATA[ 






<p>Fire departments generate a lot of data: every call, every unit, every timestamp. Most of it ends up in a report that gets filed and forgotten. This blog is about putting that data to work.</p>
<section id="what-to-expect" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="what-to-expect">What to expect</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Response time analysis:</strong> where the minutes actually go, from call received to arrival on scene.</li>
<li><strong>Station and unit coverage:</strong> who can get where, and how fast.</li>
<li><strong>Demand patterns:</strong> when and where calls happen, and what that means for staffing.</li>
<li><strong>Community risk reduction:</strong> using data to target prevention where it matters most.</li>
</ul>
<p>Posts include the R code behind every chart, so you can run the same analysis on your own department’s data.</p>
</section>
<section id="a-note-on-data" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="a-note-on-data">A note on data</h2>
<p>Everything published here uses aggregated, de-identified, public, or simulated data. Incident addresses and patient information never appear on this site.</p>


<!-- -->

</section>

